 points ahead of independent Ken Block, with the Republican nominee drawing off votes that would otherwise go to Block. Ratings and markets still treat Rhode Island as a comfortable Democratic hold, but Block's late momentum and thin polling of the true matchup warrant more uncertainty than a typical Safe D rating.", "watch": "If Guckian withdraws and consolidates his support behind Block, or a new poll shows the gap shrinking further, the race could tighten toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot.", "Analyst margin of +9.0 was limited to +10.7 (max 10 points from the baseline)."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governor's race is an open seat after Dan McKee lost renomination, and Democrat Helena Foulkes has taken clear control of it. Polls give her a large lead over Republican Aaron Guckian, but the sharper test is independent Ken Block, a two-time statewide candidate whom the latest Emerson poll places nine points behind. Block has gained ground, picked up the endorsement of a MAGA-aligned candidate, and would benefit if Guckian withdrew, though Guckian has resisted calls to drop out. The Democratic Governors Association is running ads on Foulkes's behalf.\n\nEvery major rating outlet still calls the race solidly Democratic, and prediction markets price Foulkes's chances around 95 percent or better. The state's Democratic lean, the split anti-Foulkes vote, and Block's two previous losses all point the same way. A single late poll of the true matchup keeps uncertainty elevated, but Foulkes remains clearly favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 18.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.997, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9964, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9953, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9924, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9959